@* A table that scrolls itself rather than the page.

   Twenty-six tables across thirteen pages, in seven different class combinations, and not one of
   them had a scroll wrapper — so whether a page slid sideways on a phone came down to how many
   columns it happened to need. The wrapper, the density, the numerals and the keyboard affordance
   are all decided here.

   tabindex is not optional: a scroll container whose content is plain text cannot be reached by
   keyboard otherwise, since Chrome does not make scrollers focusable the way Firefox does. It also
   needs a name, or a screen reader announces an unlabelled region. *@

<div class="table-responsive" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="@Label">
    <table class="table table-sm align-middle @(Fit ? "w-auto" : null) @Class">
        @ChildContent
    </table>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Names the scrollable region. Say what the rows are, not that it is a table.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Label { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>Shrink to the width of the content instead of filling the page.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Fit { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
